Lab 10: Automate Configuration of Ansible Automation Platform
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Use Ansible playbooks to automate the configuration of Ansible Automation Platform components
Configure Automation Controller settings using Infrastructure as Code principles via the Controller API
Automate the setup of execution environments and organizational structures in Automation Controller
Integrate an external Git repository as a Controller project using API-driven automation
Create a reusable maintenance playbook for platform housekeeping tasks
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Ansible playbooks and YAML syntax
Familiarity with Linux command line operations
Knowledge of REST APIs and HTTP methods (GET, POST, authentication headers)
Understanding of Git version control concepts
Basic networking concepts (ports, firewalls, DNS)
Required Knowledge Areas
Ansible Fundamentals: variables, tasks, handlers, and modules
YAML Structure: proper indentation and syntax
API Concepts: authentication, endpoints, and HTTP status codes
Software Requirements
This lab assumes you already have access to a running Ansible Automation Controller instance (for example, a lab-provided instance or a local trial installation) reachable over HTTPS, along with a control node from which to run Ansible. On the control node, install the required tooling:

# Verify Ansible is installed; install if missing
ansible --version || sudo dnf install -y ansible-core

# Install the Python requests library used by the uri module for JSON handling
pip3 install --user requests

# Install the community.general collection (provides useful modules used below)
ansible-galaxy collection install community.general
Set the following environment variables in your shell before starting, replacing the values with your actual Controller connection details:

export CONTROLLER_HOST="https://controller.lab.local"
export CONTROLLER_USER="admin"
export CONTROLLER_PASSWORD="your-admin-password"
Lab Environment Setup
Good News! Al Nafi provides pre-configured Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your environment. No need to build your own virtual machines or install software from scratch.

Your Lab Environment Includes
Control Node: a Linux system with Ansible Core installed, used to run all playbooks in this lab
Automation Controller Node: a reachable Automation Controller instance with a valid admin account
All nodes are networked and accessible via SSH with pre-configured key authentication. This lab treats the Controller as a system to configure entirely through its REST API using Ansible's uri module, which is the supported, non-destructive way to automate Controller setup without touching its internal filesystem or services directly.

Task 1: Prepare the Automation Project and Validate Connectivity
Subtask 1.1: Create the Project Structure
Connect to your control node and set up a working directory:

ssh ansible-control

mkdir -p ~/automation-platform-config/{playbooks,templates,group_vars}
cd ~/automation-platform-config
Create a variables file that stores your Controller connection details (do not commit real passwords to version control in a real environment; this is a lab-only convenience):

cat > group_vars/all.yml << 'EOF'
---
controller_host: "{{ lookup('env', 'CONTROLLER_HOST') }}"
controller_username: "{{ lookup('env', 'CONTROLLER_USER') }}"
controller_password: "{{ lookup('env', 'CONTROLLER_PASSWORD') }}"
controller_validate_certs: false
lab_organization_name: "Lab Organization"
lab_project_name: "ansible-examples"
lab_project_git_url: "https://github.com/ansible/ansible-examples.git"
lab_project_git_branch: "master"
EOF
Subtask 1.2: Verify API Connectivity
Create a small playbook to confirm you can reach the Controller API and authenticate successfully:

cat > playbooks/00-verify-connectivity.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Verify Automation Controller API connectivity
  hosts: localhost
  connection: local
  gather_facts: no
  vars_files:
    - ../group_vars/all.yml

  tasks:
    - name: Confirm required variables are set
      assert:
        that:
          - controller_host | length > 0
          - controller_username | length > 0
          - controller_password | length > 0
        fail_msg: "CONTROLLER_HOST, CONTROLLER_USER, and CONTROLLER_PASSWORD must be exported before running this playbook"

    - name: Ping the Controller API
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/ping/"
        method: GET
        user: "{{ controller_username }}"
        password: "{{ controller_password }}"
        force_basic_auth: yes
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        status_code: 200
      register: ping_result

    - name: Display Controller version information
      debug:
        msg:
          - "Controller reachable: {{ controller_host }}"
          - "Reported version: {{ ping_result.json.version }}"
          - "Active node: {{ ping_result.json.active_node }}"
EOF
Run it:

ansible-playbook playbooks/00-verify-connectivity.yml
Deliverable: The playbook completes with ok=3 changed=0 failed=0 and the debug output shows a version string from ping_result.json.version and a non-empty active_node. If the play fails at the assert step, re-export your environment variables; if it fails at the ping step, verify network connectivity and credentials to the Controller.

Task 2: Automate Organization, Credential, and Project Creation
Subtask 2.1: Create the Organization and Team Structure
Create a playbook that defines a lab organization using the Controller API. This uses POST with status_code: [201, 400] because a 400 response indicates the object already exists (Controller enforces unique names), which we treat as an acceptable, idempotent outcome for this lab.

cat > playbooks/01-configure-organization.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Configure organization structure in Automation Controller
  hosts: localhost
  connection: local
  gather_facts: no
  vars_files:
    - ../group_vars/all.yml

  tasks:
    - name: Authenticate and obtain an OAuth2 token
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/tokens/"
        method: POST
        user: "{{ controller_username }}"
        password: "{{ controller_password }}"
        force_basic_auth: yes
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        body_format: json
        body:
          description: "Lab 10 automation token"
          scope: "write"
        status_code: 201
      register: token_result

    - name: Set authentication header fact for subsequent requests
      set_fact:
        auth_header:
          Authorization: "Bearer {{ token_result.json.token }}"

    - name: Create the lab organization
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/organizations/"
        method: POST
        headers: "{{ auth_header }}"
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        body_format: json
        body:
          name: "{{ lab_organization_name }}"
          description: "Organization created for Lab 10 automation exercises"
        status_code: [201, 400]
      register: org_create_result

    - name: Look up the organization ID (handles both create and already-exists cases)
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/organizations/?name={{ lab_organization_name | urlencode }}"
        method: GET
        headers: "{{ auth_header }}"
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        status_code: 200
      register: org_lookup_result

    - name: Store the organization ID as a fact
      set_fact:
        lab_organization_id: "{{ org_lookup_result.json.results[0].id }}"

    - name: Display organization details
      debug:
        msg:
          - "Organization name: {{ lab_organization_name }}"
          - "Organization ID: {{ lab_organization_id }}"
EOF
Run it:

ansible-playbook playbooks/01-configure-organization.yml
Deliverable: The debug task prints a numeric Organization ID. Running the playbook a second time still succeeds (idempotent), since the create step returns 400 on the second run but the lookup step always resolves the ID.

Subtask 2.2: Create a Project Linked to an External Git Repository
Extend the automation to register the ansible-examples public Git repository as a Controller Project. This demonstrates external repository integration using only the API.

cat > playbooks/02-configure-project.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Integrate external Git repository as a Controller project
  hosts: localhost
  connection: local
  gather_facts: no
  vars_files:
    - ../group_vars/all.yml

  tasks:
    - name: Authenticate and obtain an OAuth2 token
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/tokens/"
        method: POST
        user: "{{ controller_username }}"
        password: "{{ controller_password }}"
        force_basic_auth: yes
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        body_format: json
        body:
          description: "Lab 10 project automation token"
          scope: "write"
        status_code: 201
      register: token_result

    - name: Set authentication header fact
      set_fact:
        auth_header:
          Authorization: "Bearer {{ token_result.json.token }}"

    - name: Look up the organization ID
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/organizations/?name={{ lab_organization_name | urlencode }}"
        method: GET
        headers: "{{ auth_header }}"
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        status_code: 200
      register: org_lookup_result

    - name: Fail with a clear message if the organization does not exist yet
      assert:
        that:
          - org_lookup_result.json.count > 0
        fail_msg: "Organization '{{ lab_organization_name }}' not found. Run 01-configure-organization.yml first."

    - name: Store the organization ID as a fact
      set_fact:
        lab_organization_id: "{{ org_lookup_result.json.results[0].id }}"

    - name: Create the project from the external Git repository
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/projects/"
        method: POST
        headers: "{{ auth_header }}"
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        body_format: json
        body:
          name: "{{ lab_project_name }}"
          description: "Project synced from an external Git repository for Lab 10"
          organization: "{{ lab_organization_id }}"
          scm_type: "git"
          scm_url: "{{ lab_project_git_url }}"
          scm_branch: "{{ lab_project_git_branch }}"
          scm_update_on_launch: true
        status_code: [201, 400]
      register: project_create_result

    - name: Look up the project ID
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/projects/?name={{ lab_project_name | urlencode }}"
        method: GET
        headers: "{{ auth_header }}"
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        status_code: 200
      register: project_lookup_result

    - name: Store the project ID as a fact
      set_fact:
        lab_project_id: "{{ project_lookup_result.json.results[0].id }}"

    - name: Trigger a project sync from the Git repository
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/projects/{{ lab_project_id }}/update/"
        method: POST
        headers: "{{ auth_header }}"
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        status_code: [202, 405]
      register: project_sync_result

    - name: Wait for the project sync to complete
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/projects/{{ lab_project_id }}/"
        method: GET
        headers: "{{ auth_header }}"
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        status_code: 200
      register: project_status_result
      until: project_status_result.json.status in ['successful', 'failed', 'canceled']
      retries: 20
      delay: 5

    - name: Display project sync outcome
      debug:
        msg:
          - "Project name: {{ lab_project_name }}"
          - "Project ID: {{ lab_project_id }}"
          - "Sync status: {{ project_status_result.json.status }}"
EOF
Run it:

ansible-playbook playbooks/02-configure-project.yml
Deliverable: The final debug output shows Sync status: successful. You can confirm this independently by logging into the Controller web UI, navigating to Projects, and seeing ansible-examples listed with a green (successful) last sync indicator.

Task 3: Automate Recurring Platform Maintenance Checks
Subtask 3.1: Create a Maintenance Check Playbook
Rather than modifying the Controller's internal files directly (which is unsupported and can break the platform), this task automates routine health checks through the API and produces a maintenance report. This mirrors real-world practice, where day-2 operations on Automation Controller are performed through its API and command-line utilities, not by hand-editing service internals.

cat > playbooks/03-maintenance-check.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Perform Automation Controller maintenance health checks
  hosts: localhost
  connection: local
  gather_facts: yes
  vars_files:
    - ../group_vars/all.yml
  vars:
    report_dir: "{{ playbook_dir }}/../reports"

  tasks:
    - name: Ensure the reports directory exists
      file:
        path: "{{ report_dir }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'

    - name: Authenticate and obtain an OAuth2 token
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/tokens/"
        method: POST
        user: "{{ controller_username }}"
        password: "{{ controller_password }}"
        force_basic_auth: yes
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        body_format: json
        body:
          description: "Lab 10 maintenance token"
          scope: "write"
        status_code: 201
      register: token_result

    - name: Set authentication header fact
      set_fact:
        auth_header:
          Authorization: "Bearer {{ token_result.json.token }}"

    - name: Query overall Controller health via the ping endpoint
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/ping/"
        method: GET
        headers: "{{ auth_header }}"
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        status_code: 200
      register: health_check

    - name: Query recent job status summary
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/jobs/?order_by=-finished&page_size=5"
        method: GET
        headers: "{{ auth_header }}"
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        status_code: 200
      register: recent_jobs

    - name: Query project sync health
      uri:
        url: "{{ controller_host }}/api/v2/projects/"
        method: GET
        headers: "{{ auth_header }}"
        validate_certs: "{{ controller_validate_certs }}"
        status_code: 200
      register: all_projects

    - name: Identify projects with a failed last sync
      set_fact:
        failed_projects: "{{ all_projects.json.results | selectattr('status', 'equalto', 'failed') | map(attribute='name') | list }}"

    - name: Generate maintenance report
      template:
        src: ../templates/maintenance_report.j2
        dest: "{{ report_dir }}/maintenance_report_{{ ansible_date_time.date }}.txt"
        mode: '0644'

    - name: Display report location
      debug:
        msg: "Maintenance report written to {{ report_dir }}/maintenance_report_{{ ansible_date_time.date }}.txt"
EOF
Subtask 3.2: Create the Maintenance Report Template
Create the Jinja2 template referenced by the playbook above:

cat > templates/maintenance_report.j2 << 'EOF'
=================================================================
Ansible Automation Controller Maintenance Report
Generated: {{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }}
Controller: {{ controller_host }}
=================================================================

CONTROLLER HEALTH
-----------------
API reachable: {{ 'YES' if health_check.status == 200 else 'NO' }}
Reported version: {{ health_check.json.version }}
Active node: {{ health_check.json.active_node }}

RECENT JOB ACTIVITY (last 5 jobs)
----------------------------------
{% for job in recent_jobs.json.results %}
- ID: {{ job.id }} | Name: {{ job.name }} | Status: {{ job.status }} | Finished: {{ job.finished }}
{% endfor %}
{% if recent_jobs.json.results | length == 0 %}
No jobs have been run on this Controller yet.
{% endif %}

PROJECT SYNC STATUS
--------------------
Total projects: {{ all_projects.json.count }}
{% if failed_projects | length > 0 %}
Projects with failed sync: {{ failed_projects | join(', ') }}
{% else %}
No projects currently show a failed sync status.
{% endif %}

=================================================================
End of report
=================================================================
EOF
Subtask 3.3: Run the Maintenance Playbook and Review the Report
ansible-playbook playbooks/03-maintenance-check.yml

# Review the generated report
cat reports/maintenance_report_*.txt
Deliverable: A file named reports/maintenance_report_<date>.txt exists and contains four populated sections (Controller Health, Recent Job Activity, Project Sync Status, and a closing footer). The Reported version line must show a real version string returned by the Controller, and Total projects must be at least 1 (the ansible-examples project created in Task 2).

Expected Outcomes
A working set of idempotent Ansible playbooks that configure an Automation Controller organization and project purely through its REST API
A verified external Git repository (ansible-examples) registered and successfully synced as a Controller project
A repeatable maintenance playbook that produces a timestamped, human-readable health report you can schedule or re-run on demand
Troubleshooting
Issue 1: uri tasks fail with a certificate verification error. Symptom: task output includes certificate verify failed or SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED. Diagnostic hint: check whether your Controller uses a self-signed certificate, and confirm that controller_validate_certs in group_vars/all.yml is set appropriately for your lab environment; do not disable certificate validation outside of an isolated lab.

Issue 2: Organization or project lookup returns an empty results list. Symptom: a subsequent task fails with an "index out of range" or undefined-attribute error when accessing results[0]. Diagnostic hint: query the endpoint manually with the GET request from the failing task (for example, using curl with the same URL and auth header) to confirm whether the object exists and whether the name filter matches exactly, including case and whitespace.

Conclusion
In this lab, you automated the configuration of an Ansible Automation Controller environment entirely through its documented REST API, using idempotent Ansible playbooks instead of manual clicks or unsupported file edits. You created an organization, registered and synced an external Git repository as a project, and built a maintenance playbook that queries Controller health, recent job activity, and project sync status to generate a timestamped report. Along the way you practiced core Infrastructure as Code patterns: token-based authentication, idempotent create-or-lookup logic, waiting on asynchronous operations, and using Jinja2 templates to produce operational reports. From here, consider extending the maintenance playbook to alert on failed job or project statuses, integrating it into a scheduled job template inside the Controller itself, and applying the same API-driven approach to automate credential, inventory, and job template management.
